{"activity_text": "Anti-Gram+", "article_id": "", "assay_text": "Unknown", "assay_type": "", "comments_text": "Sequence analysis: APD analysis reveals that the sequence of this peptide most resembles (44.4% similarity) Baceridin. Originally identified as a sex pheromone (Nakayama J et al., 1995, Isolation and Structure of the Enterococcus faecalis Sex Pheromone, cOB1, That Induces Conjugal Transfer of the Hemolysin/Bacteriocin Plasmids, pOB1 and pYI1 (plasmid-encoded), Japan Society of Biosci. Biotech. and Agrochem, Tokyo, pp. 703-705), Activity: cOB1 of Commensal E. faecalis can restrict the growth of multidrug-resistant E. faecalis V583 (MIC 22 pg/mL) in the gut at an extremely low concentration. It can form amyloid-like structures ( Gour et al., 2019). MOA:bacteria: The killing mechanism is under investigation but apparently differs from the classic AMPs that target membranes. AMP significance: This study indicates the importance of maintaining the commensal population as a means of preventing infection. Update 4/2016; 12/2024.", "concentration": "", "cytotoxicity_text": "", "hemolytic_activity_text": "", "measure_group": "", "measure_value": "", "pubmed_id": "26039987", "record_granularity": "entry_text", "reference_text": "Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2015 Jun 9;112(23):7273-8. PubMed", "sequence_key": "APD6:AP02649", "source_id": "AP02649", "source_numeric_id": "02649", "source_path": "APD6/apd6_export/structured/peptides.csv", "source_record_id": "AP02649", "source_table": "peptides.csv", "stability_text": "", "subject_name": "", "target_organism_text": "", "title": "Pheromone killing of multidrug-resistant Enterococcus faecalis V583 by native commensal strains", "unit": "", "﻿database": "APD6"}
